WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.980
Hey Crazies.

00:00:00.980 --> 00:00:03.040
Sometimes math is unavoidable.

00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.840
Without it, you miss out on important insights,

00:00:05.840 --> 00:00:09.960
so what if, instead of avoiding math, we try to visualize it?

00:00:09.960 --> 00:00:12.940
I want to give you a glimpse inside my head
for a few minutes.

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:20.200
This episode was made possible by generous supporters on Patreon.

00:00:20.200 --> 00:00:24.800
Ok, so the math I want to help you visualize today is divergence and curl,

00:00:24.800 --> 00:00:26.260
but this isn’t a math channel.

00:00:26.260 --> 00:00:27.680
It’s a science channel.

00:00:27.680 --> 00:00:29.220
We need a context!

00:00:29.220 --> 00:00:33.880
Fluid flow seems like an obvious choice, but those aren’t always simple like this.

00:00:33.880 --> 00:00:36.300
Sometimes they’re rather complicated

00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:41.300
and, if we’re being honest, I don’t really understand fluid mechanics as much as I’d like to.

00:00:41.300 --> 00:00:43.800
I’m trying to teach myself, but that takes time,

00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:48.240
so we’ll reserve fluid animations like this for analogies only.

00:00:48.240 --> 00:00:52.600
Let’s focus on something I happen to know a lot about: the electromagnetic field,

00:00:52.600 --> 00:00:55.500
which is actually a combination of two different fields:

00:00:55.500 --> 00:01:00.500
An electric field shown in blue and a magnetic
field shown in orange.

00:01:00.500 --> 00:01:04.000
We did a whole series on how we discovered those fields and what they do,

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:08.000
but I didn’t talk about divergence or curl at all and I think that was a mistake.

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:09.560
So it’s time to fix it!

00:01:09.560 --> 00:01:12.299
We have two behaviors: divergence and curl

00:01:12.300 --> 00:01:15.480
acting on two different fields: electric and magnetic.

00:01:15.480 --> 00:01:17.700
That means we need four equations.

00:01:17.700 --> 00:01:21.000
They look something like this: the Maxwell-Heaviside equations,

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:23.620
which most people just call Maxwell’s equations.

00:01:23.620 --> 00:01:24.820
Don’t get my started.

00:01:24.820 --> 00:01:27.640
But these constants are just there to make the units work out.

00:01:27.640 --> 00:01:31.180
They’re not actually important for understanding the equations,

00:01:31.180 --> 00:01:33.380
so I’ll just set them all equal to one.

00:01:33.380 --> 00:01:34.460
I do what I want!

00:01:34.460 --> 00:01:39.000
Alright, as we go through these one at a time, we’ll look at the equation first, and

00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:41.660
then we’ll see what we can do about making it more visual.

00:01:41.660 --> 00:01:44.640
Let’s start with this one, also known as Gauss’s law.

00:01:44.649 --> 00:01:48.219
It says the divergence of the electric field at some point

00:01:48.220 --> 00:01:51.340
equals the charge density at that same point.

00:01:51.340 --> 00:01:55.760
In other words, the reason the electric field isn’t just zero everywhere

00:01:55.760 --> 00:01:58.180
is because electric charge exists.

00:01:58.180 --> 00:02:01.740
But this law tells us something about the shape of the field too.

00:02:01.740 --> 00:02:05.280
Say we draw an imaginary sphere around some region of space.

00:02:05.280 --> 00:02:10.180
The outer surface of that sphere has electric field arrows sticking in or out of it.

00:02:10.180 --> 00:02:13.860
Since the sphere is imaginary, we can make
it any size we’d like.

00:02:13.860 --> 00:02:18.640
But, to find the divergence, we need to make that sphere really small,

00:02:18.640 --> 00:02:22.920
like, just a bit larger the size of a single point kind of small.

00:02:22.920 --> 00:02:25.560
You’re such a little adorable sphere, yes you are.

00:02:25.560 --> 00:02:26.600
Yes you are.

00:02:27.480 --> 00:02:29.040
(cough) Anyway.

00:02:29.400 --> 00:02:34.720
The divergence of a field tells us how much that field points away from that tiny sphere.

00:02:34.720 --> 00:02:39.920
If the field points into the sphere as much as it points out, then the divergence is zero.

00:02:39.920 --> 00:02:43.040
Gauss’s law tells us there’s no charge at that point.

00:02:43.040 --> 00:02:48.140
If the field points more out of that sphere than in, the divergence is a positive number.

00:02:48.140 --> 00:02:51.320
Gauss’s law tells us there’s positive charge at that point.

00:02:51.320 --> 00:02:56.060
If the field points more into the sphere than out, the divergence is a negative number.

00:02:56.060 --> 00:02:59.300
Gauss’s law tells us there’s negative
charge at that point.

00:02:59.300 --> 00:03:02.760
Electric charge is the reason the field looks like that.

00:03:02.760 --> 00:03:05.860
Of course, that’s all very static and a little boring.

00:03:05.860 --> 00:03:11.540
If it helps you can imagine this electric field is the velocity field for some fluid instead.

00:03:11.540 --> 00:03:16.780
That way it’s easier to see the inward and outward directions at any particular place,

00:03:16.780 --> 00:03:20.160
but it’s important to remember this is just a visual tool.

00:03:20.160 --> 00:03:22.740
Nothing is actually flowing in this picture.

00:03:22.740 --> 00:03:25.800
There’s no electromagnetic fluid. It’s not a thing!

00:03:25.800 --> 00:03:28.920
We can interpret the other divergence equation the same way.

00:03:28.920 --> 00:03:34.020
It says the divergence of the magnetic field at some point is always equal to zero.

00:03:34.020 --> 00:03:37.980
So, unlike with electricity, there’s no such thing as magnetic charge.

00:03:37.980 --> 00:03:40.860
In other words, there are no magnetic monopoles.

00:03:40.860 --> 00:03:44.720
Magnetic poles, like north and south, always come in pairs.

00:03:44.720 --> 00:03:47.560
Consider the magnetic field around this bar magnet.

00:03:47.560 --> 00:03:52.560
Using that same imaginary sphere, the divergence is how much the field points away.

00:03:52.560 --> 00:03:55.740
Since the divergence of the magnetic field is always zero,

00:03:55.740 --> 00:04:00.840
there is always just as much field pointed into the sphere as there is pointed out of it,

00:04:00.840 --> 00:04:03.220
no matter where we put the sphere.

00:04:03.220 --> 00:04:06.240
This becomes pretty obvious using the fluid analogy.

00:04:06.240 --> 00:04:10.240
Every streamline that goes into the sphere comes back out.

00:04:10.240 --> 00:04:12.200
That’s all there is to divergence.

00:04:12.200 --> 00:04:13.900
The curl, on the other hand.

00:04:16.980 --> 00:04:18.420
Why does this keep happening?!?

00:04:18.420 --> 00:04:20.640
Curl is a different story.

00:04:20.640 --> 00:04:24.620
Your first instinct might be to imagine those closed fluid loops again

00:04:24.620 --> 00:04:26.860
and that’s certainly an example of a curl.

00:04:26.860 --> 00:04:30.880
But this flow has a curl too without loops of any kind.

00:04:30.880 --> 00:04:34.360
That’s because curl isn’t really about the field itself.

00:04:34.360 --> 00:04:36.620
It’s about what that field might cause.

00:04:36.620 --> 00:04:42.160
In this fluid example, say we draw a tiny
imaginary circle somewhere on the screen.

00:04:42.160 --> 00:04:44.680
(baby noises)

00:04:44.680 --> 00:04:46.340
Oh come on, man!

00:04:46.340 --> 00:04:49.320
If we imagine some kind of paddle attached to that circle,

00:04:49.320 --> 00:04:53.180
the difference in flow between the top and bottom will cause it to spin.

00:04:53.180 --> 00:04:57.680
So, even though the field itself doesn’t circulate, the things inside it will.

00:04:57.680 --> 00:05:00.000
Now let’s get back to electromagnetism.

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:03.880
Faraday’s law says the curl of the electric
field at some point

00:05:03.880 --> 00:05:07.900
equals the negative change in the magnetic
field at that same point.

00:05:07.900 --> 00:05:10.680
Say we draw a tiny imaginary circle again

00:05:10.680 --> 00:05:13.800
and we notice a magnetic field passing through that circle.

00:05:13.800 --> 00:05:16.220
If that field is changing over time,

00:05:16.220 --> 00:05:21.920
Faraday’s law tells us there must be an electric field that curls around the imaginary circle.

00:05:21.920 --> 00:05:26.620
The curl of that electric field will be opposite the change in the magnetic field.

00:05:26.620 --> 00:05:29.620
But remember, the curl isn’t about the shape of the field.

00:05:29.620 --> 00:05:32.000
It’s about what that field might cause.

00:05:32.000 --> 00:05:37.260
If our imaginary circle happens to be a metal ring, we’ll get a circulating current.

00:05:37.260 --> 00:05:39.360
That result is what matters.

00:05:39.360 --> 00:05:41.220
So where was I again?

00:05:41.220 --> 00:05:46.180
Divergence of electric field, divergence of magnetic field, curl of electric field.

00:05:46.180 --> 00:05:47.120
Oh, right!

00:05:47.120 --> 00:05:50.360
There’s one equation left: the curl of the magnetic field.

00:05:50.360 --> 00:05:54.940
It’s pretty obvious from the bar magnet example that the magnetic field curls.

00:05:54.940 --> 00:05:58.560
But let’s take a look at Ampere’s law and see what it actually says.

00:05:58.560 --> 00:06:03.300
A curl in the magnetic field at some point can happen for one of two reasons:

00:06:03.300 --> 00:06:05.820
An electric current passing through that point

00:06:05.820 --> 00:06:08.740
or a changing electric field at that point.

00:06:08.740 --> 00:06:12.240
It’s the longest of the four equations, which is why I saved it for last.

00:06:12.240 --> 00:06:13.720
Ahhhh!

00:06:13.720 --> 00:06:15.760
Don’t panic! We can handle this!

00:06:15.760 --> 00:06:18.200
Say we’ve got an electric current in a wire.

00:06:18.200 --> 00:06:22.100
If we draw our imaginary circle so electric current passes through it,

00:06:22.100 --> 00:06:26.240
Ampere’s law says the magnetic field should curl around that circle.

00:06:26.240 --> 00:06:30.040
Now let’s say, somewhere along this wire, we’ve got a capacitor.

00:06:30.040 --> 00:06:34.840
It’ll act like a gap in the circuit, which means no steady current, which means no field.

00:06:34.840 --> 00:06:37.680
Unless that current is alternating!

00:06:37.680 --> 00:06:40.980
(dramatic music)

00:06:40.980 --> 00:06:42.140
(laugh)

00:06:42.140 --> 00:06:46.780
Alternating current, or AC, opens up a lot of possibilities for us.

00:06:46.780 --> 00:06:50.520
Since the current in the wires is changing on either side of the capacitor,

00:06:50.520 --> 00:06:54.120
the electric field inside the capacitor is also changing.

00:06:54.120 --> 00:06:59.260
That changing electric field creates a curl in the magnetic field around our circle,

00:06:59.260 --> 00:07:01.380
just like the current does in the wire.

00:07:01.380 --> 00:07:05.940
This keeps the behavior of the magnetic field smooth across the entire picture.

00:07:05.940 --> 00:07:09.660
There’s a curl in the magnetic field all
along the circuit chain.

00:07:09.660 --> 00:07:12.640
So what’s the best way to visualize Maxwell’s equations?

00:07:12.640 --> 00:07:16.620
Using tiny imaginary shapes and a little imaginary fluid,

00:07:16.620 --> 00:07:21.830
divergence tells us how much a field points toward or away from a point in space.

00:07:21.830 --> 00:07:24.930
For electric fields, that’s caused by charges.

00:07:24.930 --> 00:07:29.620
For magnetic fields, it’s always zero, so we know magnetic poles always come in pairs.

00:07:29.620 --> 00:07:34.540
Curl tells us which way something inside the field might spin or circulate.

00:07:34.540 --> 00:07:38.760
In electrodynamics, that means we’ll probably get some kind of electric current.

00:07:38.760 --> 00:07:43.480
Just remember, those shapes are imaginary and that fluid isn’t really there.

00:07:43.480 --> 00:07:46.140
They’re just tools to help us visualize the math.

00:07:46.140 --> 00:07:47.340
So what do you think?

00:07:47.340 --> 00:07:49.120
Should I do more videos like this?

00:07:49.120 --> 00:07:50.820
Please share your thoughts in the comments.

00:07:50.820 --> 00:07:53.880
Also, maybe, answer the poll that pops up over here somewhere.

00:07:53.880 --> 00:07:57.080
If you want to see the actual math of divergence and curl,

00:07:57.080 --> 00:07:59.820
there’s a whole chapter in my eBook on vector calculus.

00:07:59.820 --> 00:08:02.540
Big news though! It’s a physical book now!

00:08:02.540 --> 00:08:03.960
What?!

00:08:03.960 --> 00:08:05.420
I know, right? It’s crazy.

00:08:05.420 --> 00:08:07.780
It makes it feel so much more real to me.

00:08:07.780 --> 00:08:12.120
It’s available through Lulu dot com, which helped me keep the price reasonable.

00:08:12.120 --> 00:08:16.080
Links for both the eBook version and the physical version can be found in the doobly-doo.

00:08:16.080 --> 00:08:17.820
Thanks for liking and sharing this video.

00:08:17.820 --> 00:08:20.220
Don’t forget to subscribe if you’d like to keep up with us.

00:08:20.220 --> 00:08:23.620
And until next time, remember, it’s OK to be a little crazy.

00:08:25.460 --> 00:08:30.540
Chaos Fever mentioned that, in Greece, they call the nabla symbol “upside down delta”

00:08:30.540 --> 00:08:33.360
That’s kind of cool actually because that’s exactly what it is,

00:08:33.360 --> 00:08:37.000
but when I type that Greek word into Google Translate, it says it means lazy.

00:08:37.000 --> 00:08:39.620
I wonder if they’re trying to say an upside
down delta is lazy.

00:08:39.620 --> 00:08:41.300
Anyway, thanks for watching!

